{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792504969,"nonce":"3VuSbfCXyLvieKQZJWiAczKZz+k=","title":"Compiler: support top-level initialization without a main function","message":"Languages with top-level statements currently have to manufacture a named entry function. TypeScript emits ts$main even for pure libraries; linking a snapshot with that name can replace the importing program's own entry. Function-only modules already lower without main, but shared TypedAST/HIR lack an explicit top-level execution contract.\n\nAdd a name-free typed initializer declaration and shared lowering to internal module initialization code. Carry ordered initializer roots through HIR, snapshots and deferred imports. TieredRuntime must run reachable module initializers in dependency order, once per installed initializer, before callers enter the module, while preserving interpreter-first execution and ordinary tier promotion. Empty/function-only programs must not need an entry or invent an initializer. Keep explicit main functions and host-declared entry points working.\n\nMove TypeScript top-level blocks to this shared path and remove its ts$main declaration/call requirement. Preserve strict floating-point annotations, static release analysis, typed GEP specialization and source diagnostics. Cover declaration-only and empty files, top-level ordering and side effects, imports/snapshot relocation, initialization failure, repeat imports/compiles, closed-program reachability, and interpreter/Cranelift/LLVM execution. Leave full TypeScript module resolution/export semantics in 0c26889a7cd2c4060eb94ef93fd808c2857089dbf714735ca52b5d071bdb1564.","files":null}]}